Below is a compact, self‑contained C# example that shows:

1. **A custom type (`Resource`) that implements `IDisposable`.**  
   It keeps an “unmanaged”‑style resource (here just a string identifier) and logs when it is disposed.

2. **Two `using` blocks** – one classic `using` syntax and one the newer C# 8/9 `using var` syntax – to demonstrate how `Dispose()` is called automatically when the block ends.

3. **Manual `Dispose()`** – how you could dispose the object yourself.

```csharp
using System;

namespace DisposableDemo
{
    /// <summary>
    /// A very simple class that pretends to own an unmanaged resource.
    /// Implements IDisposable so callers can clean it up deterministically.
    /// </summary>
    sealed class Resource : IDisposable
    {
        private readonly string _name;
        private bool _disposed;

        public Resource(string name)
        {
            _name = name;
            Console.WriteLine($"[Resource {_name}] Constructor: acquiring resource.");
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// Release the "resource".
        /// </summary>
        public void Dispose()
        {
            // Guard against multiple calls
            if (_disposed) return;

            // Here you would release unmanaged resources (e.g., file handles, DB connections)
            Console.WriteLine($"[Resource {_name}] Dispose: releasing resource.");

            // Suppress finalization if a finalizer were defined
            GC.SuppressFinalize(this);
            _disposed = true;
        }

        ~Resource()
        {
            // Finalizer is a safety net – normally shouldn't be needed if Dispose is called
            if (!_disposed)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"[Resource {_name}] Finalizer: disposing in GC.");
                Dispose();
            }
        }

        public void DoWork()
        {
            if (_disposed)
                throw new ObjectDisposedException(nameof(Resource));

            Console.WriteLine($"[Resource {_name}] Doing some work...");
        }
    }

    class Program
    {
        static void Main()
        {
            // Classic using syntax – Dispose is called automatically at the end of the block.
            using (var res1 = new Resource("First"))
            {
                res1.DoWork();
                // You can also call Dispose() manually inside the block if you want early cleanup.
                // res1.Dispose();
            } // Dispose() is called here automatically.

            Console.WriteLine();

            // C# 8+ "using var" syntax – the variable is disposed when it goes out of scope.
            using var res2 = new Resource("Second");
            res2.DoWork();

            // If you want to dispose earlier, just call Dispose() manually.
            res2.Dispose();

            // At this point res2 has been disposed, so further calls would throw.
            // res2.DoWork(); // would throw ObjectDisposedException

            Console.WriteLine("\nPress any key to exit...");
            Console.ReadKey();
        }
    }
}
```

### What the program does

1. **`Resource` construction** prints a message indicating the resource is acquired.  
2. **`DoWork()`** shows normal usage while the resource is alive.  
3. When the `using` block ends (or when `Dispose()` is called manually), the resource is released and a message is printed.  
4. The finalizer (`~Resource`) is there just to illustrate that if `Dispose()` were forgotten, the garbage collector would still clean up the resource—but you should always prefer deterministic disposal with `using`.

Compile and run this code; you'll see the console output illustrating the lifecycle of the disposable objects.
